## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical on-device data structure with incremental updates, offline-first UX, and a clear 12‑month cost plan, including privacy considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Local inverted index and incremental updates
- Offline-first data paths and cache invalidation
- Delta-based cloud sync and conflict resolution
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, memory, latency, energy
- Privacy, compliance, and user consent handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal incremental inverted index (conceptual)
class InvertedIndex {
  constructor() {
    this.index = {}; // term -> Set of note IDs
  }
  addNote(id, text) {
    text.split(/\W+/).forEach(t => {
      if (!t) return;
      const w = t.toLowerCase();
      (this.index[w] ||= new Set()).add(id);
    });
  }
  search(q) {
    return q
      .toLowerCase()
      .split(/\s+/)
      .flatMap(w => Array.from(this.index[w] || []));
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate index maintenance energy and storage growth over time?
- How would you handle search latency guarantees on mid-range devices with thousands of notes?
- What privacy safeguards would you implement for on-device indexing and sync deltas?

## Why This Is Asked

Balances privacy, regulatory needs, and system load by moving KYC attestation on-device.

### Key Concepts

- On-device cryptography and attestation
- Data minimization and privacy
- Key management and rotation
- Compliance and auditability

### Code Example

```javascript
async function signAttestation(privateKey, payload) {
  const enc = new TextEncoder();
  const data = enc.encode(JSON.stringify(payload));
  const sig = await crypto.subtle.sign({name: 'ECDSA', hash: 'SHA-256'}, privateKey, data);
  return btoa(String.fromCharCode(...new Uint8Array(sig)));
}
```

### Follow-up Questions

- How to handle key compromise and revocation?
- What metrics drive break-even and rollout schedule?
- How to prevent replay attacks and ensure forward secrecy?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, new angle on CBA: on-device prefetching to cut latency, balancing storage, energy, privacy, and business impact. Tests the ability to translate UX improvements into measurable costs and decision criteria.

## Key Concepts

- On-device predictor energy/compute
- Cache budgeting and invalidation strategies
- Data transfer vs. storage costs
- Privacy/opt-in and data minimization
- Latency impact and engagement metrics
- Break-even analysis and go/no-go thresholds

## Code Example

```javascript
function calcBreakeven(params){
  const {users, cacheMB, energyPerHit, savedMsPerHit, valuePerMs, pricePerMB} = params;
  const energyCost = energyPerHit * users;
  const dataValue = savedMsPerHit * users * valuePerMs;
  const breakevenMonths = (cacheMB * pricePerMB) / ((dataValue) - energyCost);
  return breakevenMonths;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you calibrate the model without A/B tests for small pilots?
- What privacy constraints would you enforce for on-device prefetching data?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical cost-benefit analysis thinking for privacy-conscious mobile features with offline-first architecture. It tests understanding of storage trade-offs, energy budgeting, consent management, and long-term maintenance considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first storage and local geofence processing to ensure reliability without network connectivity
- Energy budgeting for continuous location monitoring and CPU usage for geofence checks
- Privacy, consent, and data minimization requirements for location-based features
- Cloud synchronization trade-offs for shared calendar data and conflict resolution
- Rollout planning with phased deployment and offline fallback behavior
- Technical debt and drift/maintenance costs over the feature lifecycle

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: register geofence, trigger locally, and schedule reminder
registerGeofence(reminder.id, reminder.coordinates, reminder.radius, async (trigger) => {
  const localReminder = await db.getReminder(reminder.id);
  if (localReminder) {
    await notificationManager.show(localReminder);
    await syncQueue.add({ reminderId: reminder.id, triggeredAt: Date.now() });
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to model cross-device costs, trade-offs, and governance for a live feature-flag infrastructure. Tests data locality, privacy, offline behavior, and rollout risk at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Local evaluation cost vs backend traffic
- Data minimization and privacy/compliance
- Rollout strategy and drift management
- Maintenance and rollback costs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple CBA calculator (illustrative)
function cba(months, backendCost, localKB, energyPerEval) {
  const backendSavings = backendCost * months;
  const localCosts = localKB * 0.002 + energyPerEval * months;
  return backendSavings - localCosts;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle offline devices during experiments?
- What telemetry would you collect to justify cost?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design and quantify edge ML systems under privacy and connectivity constraints, balancing energy, latency, and regulatory risk. It probes real-world trade-offs of federated learning with differential privacy in production environments.

## Key Concepts

- **Federated learning with local updates**: Devices train on local data and share model updates instead of raw data.
- **Secure aggregation and differential privacy**: Cryptographic protocols and noise injection protect individual contributions.
- **Energy and memory budgeting**: Quantifies the compute cost of on-device training.
- **Drift detection and validation**: Monitors model quality under privacy-preserving constraints.
- **Rollout strategy and offline fallback**: Ensures reliability when connectivity is intermittent.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple local update sketch for FL
function localUpdate(weights, localData, learningRate) {
  const gradient = computeGradient(weights, localData);
  return weights.map(w => w - learningRate * gradient);
}

// Secure aggregation (conceptual)
async function aggregateUpdates(updates, privacyBudget) {
  const clipped = updates.map(u => clipGradient(u, MAX_NORM));
  const noised = addNoise(clipped, privacyBudget);
  return average(noised);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to ground a feature in a measurable business case, translating product intuition into concrete cost and risk items for a mobile offline component.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first architecture
- Inverted index vs suffix structures
- Delta sync and storage budgets
- Privacy/compliance and consent
- Rollout strategy and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal indexing sketch (pseudo)
function indexDocs(docs) {
  const idx = {};
  for (const d of docs) {
    for (const t of tokenize(d.text)) {
      if (!idx[t]) idx[t] = [];
      idx[t].push(d.id);
    }
  }
  return idx;
}
```

